rxscene 1

# A world authored to be streamed, not to be loaded whole.
#
# Six by six cells of 32 m, so 192 m on a side, and the viewer's streaming
# bubble only ever holds a fraction of it. Bake it with:
#
#   rxworld bake runtime/scenes/streamworld.rxscene world.rxp \
#           --name streamworld --cell-size 32
#
# and drive it with `rx --world <world.rxp>`. The build does both for you; see
# runtime/CMakeLists.txt.
#
# Two kinds of thing on purpose, because the cook sorts them into two different
# domains and the runtime treats them differently:
#
#   A tile and its scatter are a Transform and a Renderable and nothing else.
#   The cook makes those instance-page rows: no ECS entity, no per-object cost,
#   drawn straight from the page. This is most of a world.
#
#   A pillar additionally carries SpawnedFrom, so it is something with
#   provenance rather than scenery. The cook makes those real ECS entities in
#   the gameplay domain, and the host draws them the same way it draws anything
#   else with a Transform and a Renderable.
#
# Guid is deliberately present on the pillars and absent from the scatter: it is
# identity the baked world replaces with its own stable id, so it must not be
# what decides which of the two a thing becomes. A scene saved by the editor has
# one on everything.
#
# Generated, then checked in: it is the authored source the cook reads, and a
# hand-written 200-entity grid would be worse in every way except provenance.

